%S A400633 262,1828,5968,15818,35230,72174,135092,241670,408156,669006,1053446,
%T A400633 1623340,2426888,3569924,5126898,7269608,10109160,13912716,18839132,
%U A400633 25291876,33489458,44023688,57187710,73827420,94325272,119866564,150937074,189165700,235157810,291113198,357769408,438055610
%N A400633 Number of semi-meanders with n crossings and winding number n-10, for n >= 10.
%C A400633 A semi-meander is a semi-infinite, self-avoiding, directed curve that crosses a line one or more times.
%C A400633 The winding number of a semi-meander is the hypothetical minimum number of self-crossings that would be created if a ray were to start at the source of the semi-infinite directed curve and extend to infinity without crossing the line.
%C A400633 The maximum winding number of a semi-meander with n crossings is n-2. Thus a(n) counts semi-meanders whose winding number is 8 less than the maximum.
%C A400633 Equivalent to a closed curve (a loop) transversely crossing a ray, considered up to continuous deformation fixing the ray, with a winding number equal to the number of crossings that would be created if the fixed end of the ray were extended to form a line.
%C A400633 Analogized to a river (a semi-meander, meander, or loop) crossing a road (a ray, line, or line segment).
%C A400633 The fifth diagonal of A400429, reading diagonals from the maximal-winding edge.
%H A400633 Hunter Hogan, <a href="/A400633/b400633.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 10..300</a>
%H A400633 Philippe Di Francesco, Olivier Golinelli and Emmanuel Guitter, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9607039">Meanders: A Direct Enumeration Approach</a>, arXiv:hep-th/9607039, 1996 (preprint).
%H A400633 Philippe Di Francesco, Olivier Golinelli and Emmanuel Guitter, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0550-3213(96)00505-6">Meanders: A Direct Enumeration Approach</a>, Nuclear Physics B 482 (1996), no. 3, 497-535.
%H A400633 Hunter Hogan, <a href="https://github.com/hunterhogan/mapFolding">mapFolding: Create, alter, and optimize algorithms to count meanders, stamp foldings, and map foldings</a>, GitHub repository, 2026.
%H A400633 Joe Sawada and Roy Li, <a href="https://doi.org/10.37236/2404">Stamp Foldings, Semi-Meanders, and Open Meanders: Fast Generation Algorithms</a>, The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics, Volume 19 No. 2 (2012), #P43 (16 pages).
%F A400633 a(n) = T(n,floor(n/2)-4), where T is the triangle A400429.
%F A400633 More generally, if D_j is the j-th diagonal of A400429, then 2*j is its least index.
%F A400633 For this sequence, j = 5 and 2*j = 10.
%F A400633 D_j(2*j) = a(10) = A005315(j) = A005316(2*j-1).
%F A400633 D_j(2*j+1) = a(11) = A005315(j+1) = A005316(2*j+1) = D_{j+1}(2*j+2) = A400634(12).
%F A400633 a(n) = floor(P(n) + (-1)^n*E(n) + C_3(n) + C_4(n) + C_8(n) + (11 - 21*cos(4*Pi*(n - 2) / 5)) / 20).
%F A400633 P(n) = (5163390*n^8 - 20844000*n^7 + 56558460*n^6 - 6784359120*n^5 - 31881482430*n^4 + 600812982000*n^3 + 5767984511820*n^2 - 68694275761200*n + 166068141699607) / 83607552000.
%F A400633 E(n) = (-476*n^6 + 6992*n^5 - 73490*n^4 + 346000*n^3 - 68012*n^2 + 519600*n + 17692977) / 9830400.
%F A400633 C_3(n) = 4*((50 + 108*n - 9*n^2)*cos(2*Pi*n / 3) + sqrt(3)*(24 - 26*n - 9*n^2)*sin(2*Pi*n / 3)) / 729.
%F A400633 m_4(t) = 2*cos(Pi*t/2).
%F A400633 C_4(n) = n*(40*m_4(n) - 33*m_4(n - 1)) / 1024.
%F A400633 m_8(t) = 2*m_4(t)*cos(Pi*t / 4).
%F A400633 C_8(n) = ((-17*n - 14)*m_8(n) + (9*n - 33)*m_8(n-1) + 60*m_8(n-2) + (-9*n - 37)*m_8(n-3)) / 128.
%e A400633 Row n=14 of A400429 is 13820,49566,35230,10948,1610,102,2, corresponding to winding numbers 0,2,4,6,8,10,12. Since n-10=4, a(14)=35230, which is the fifth-to-last value.
%Y A400633 Fifth diagonal of A400429. Cf. A005315, A005316, A400630, A400631, A400632, A400634, A400635, A400636, A400637, A400638, A400639.
